  Since there is still a lot of confusion about just what the Unabomber Man=
ifesto supports or doesn't support, I am going to post this a collection of=
 "quotable quotes" from the Manifesto which illustrate FC's main focus, whi=
ch is : human freedom, and fear of future technologies which may take it aw=
ay. =


   A personal comment: Though some of you get the impression that I wholehe=
artedly endorse FC's bombing campaign, this isn't the case. When I think ab=
out the actual deeds, I am saddened and repulsed as anyone would be. But wh=
en I read some of these passages in the manifesto I could not help but feel=
 a weird ambivalence: it was terrible what FC did, yet his rhetoric  is so =
passionate and fiery that I felt the way a listener in 1776 may have felt w=
hen Patrick Henry shouted "give me liberty or give me death". (Those guys w=
ere revolutionaries too, and they didn't mean to further their cause with f=
lowers!)
   People brush off the manifesto by saying that FC was a nut with a person=
al axe to grind. Or that 'you shouldn't listen to him because he's a murder=
er".Or that "other scholars have written better essays on the subject." Wel=
l, to me none of this changes the fact: The way our society is going, these=
 issues ought to be discussed and put before the public consciousness as lo=
udly and as often as possible!

LOSS OF FREEDOM (my own headlines added)
93. Industrial Technological society cannot be reformed in such a way as to=
 prevent it from progressively narrowing the sphere of human freedom. ...by=
 "freedom" we mean the opportunity to go thru the power proccess...without =
interference from anyone, especially from any large organization. Freedom m=
eans having power..to control the circumstances of one's own life.
111. There has been a consistent tendency...for technology to strengthen th=
e system at a high cost in individual freedom and local autonomy...freedom =
and tecnological progress are incompatible.
114. modern man is strapped down by a network of rules and regulations, and=
 his fate depends on the actions of persons remote from him whose decisions=
 he cannot influence... the result is a sense of powerlessness on the part =
of the average person.
127. A technological advance that appears not to threaten freedom often tur=
ns out to threaten it very seriously later on. For example, consider motori=
zed transport. ..when a new item of technology is introduced as an option t=
hat an individual can accept or not as he chooses, it does not necessaryily=
 remain optional.... the new technology changes society in such a way that =
people eventually find themselves forced to use it.
135  While the industrial system is sick we must destroy it. If we compromi=
se with it and let it recover from its sickness, it will eventually wipe ou=
t all of our freedom.

ALTERATION OF HUMAN NATURE
144.  Human nature has in the past put certain limits on the development of=
 societies. People could be pushed only so far and no farther. But today th=
is may be changing, because modern technology is developing ways for modify=
ing human beings
145.    Imagine a society that subjects people to conditions that make them=
 terribly unhappy, then gives them drugs to take away their unhappiness. It=
 is already happening to some extent... techniques of surveillance...method=
s of propaganda... education...psychological techniques for controlling hum=
an behavior...biological methods probably will have to be used. ...Neurolog=
y may provide other avenues for modifying the human mind. Genetic engineeri=
ng of human beings... formerly the limits of human endurance have imposed l=
imits on the deveopment of societies...industrial-tech. coeity will be able=
 to pass those limits by modifying human beings, whether by psychological m=
thocs or biological methods or both... =


BIOLOGICAL CONTROL OF BEHAVIOR
157. ... it is likely that tecnology will eventually acquire something appr=
oaching complete control over human behavior. It has been established...tha=
t human thought and behavior have a largely biological basis.  ...the fact =
that human thoughts and feelings are so open to biological intervnetion sho=
ws that the problem of controlling human behavior is mainly a technical pro=
blem...the kind of problem that is accessible to scientific attack. Giving =
the outstanding record of our society in solving tehcnical problems it is o=
verwhelmingly probable that great adavnces will be made in the control of h=
uman behavior. Will public resistance prevent the introduction of tech. con=
trol? it certainly would if an attempt were made to introduce such control =
all at once. but since control will be introduced thru a long sequence of s=
mall advances, there will be no rational and effective public resistance.
160. To those who think that all this sounds like science fiction, we point=
 out that yesterday's sf is today's fact. the Industrial REvolution has rad=
ically altered man's environment and way of life ... as tech. is increasing=
ly applied to to the human body & mind, man himself will be altered as radi=
cally as his environment and way of life has been. =


163. suppose the system survives the crisis of the next several decades. by=
 that time it will have solved...the [problem of] "socializing human beings=
" ...making people sufficiently docile so that their behavior no longer thr=
eatens the system. there will be no further obstacle to the development of =
tech. and it would presumably advance toward its logical conlusion, which i=
s complete control over everything on earth.
Human freedom will have vanished...indivuduals...will be impotent vis a vis=
 large organizations armed with supertech. and an arsenal of advanced psych=
,. and biological tools for manipulating human beings , besides instrument =
of surveillance and physical coercion.
167. Is it cruel to work for the breakdown of the system?...one has to bala=
nce struggle and death against the loss of freedom & dignity.

INTELLIGENT MACHINES, DOMESTICATED HUMANS
172. let us postulate that ...scientists succed in developing intelligent m=
achines that can do all things better than human beings can do them. ... ma=
chines might be permitted to make all of their own decisions without human =
oversight...  the fate of the human race would be at the mercy of the machi=
nes. =

  It is possible that human control  over machines may be retained. due to =
improved techniques the elite will have great controlover the masses...beca=
use human work will no longer be necessary the masses will be superfluous, =
a useless burden...it the elite is ruthless they may simply decide to exter=
minate the mass of humanity...or, the elite may play the role of good sheph=
erds...see that everyone's needs are satifsfied...of course life will be so=
 purposeless that people will have to be ...engineered...those engineeredd =
human beings may be happy... but they most certainly will not be free. They=
 will have been reduced to the status of domestic animals.

HUMAN OBSOLESCENCE: A FUTURE SCENARIO THAT LOOKS FAMILIAR
175. ...there will be an increasing surplus of human workers at the lower l=
evels of ability. On those who are employed, ever increasing demands will b=
e placed. they will need more & more training, more & more ability, will ha=
ve to be ever more reliable, conforming and docile, because they will be mo=
re & more like cells of a giant organism. their tasks will be increasingly =
specialized so that their work will.. be out of touch with the real world..=
=2E the system will have to use any means...to enginer people to be docile.=
=2E. the society may find competitiveness useful...we can imagine a furure =
soceity in which there is endless competition for positions of prestige and=
 power.

177 whatever may be left of wild nature will be reduced to remnants preserv=
ed for scientific study and kept under the supervision & management of scie=
ntistas. In the long run...it is likely that neither the human race nor any=
 other important organisms wil exist as we know them today...modifications =
will probably continue until man & other organisms have been utterly transf=
ormed.
  It is certain that technology is creating for human beings a new physical=
 & social environment radically different than that to which natural select=
ion has adapted the human race physically & phychologically....

